course: dictionary
lesson: 13630
title: Formal words: indolence
level: primary-6
stars: 2
explain: Indolence is a formal word for laziness: not wanting to work or make an effort. Writers use it in stories and old books, but in everyday talk people say «laziness». Context helps you spot it: «Sheer indolence kept the old man in his chair all day.» Sheer means complete, so the clue is that he did nothing at all.
explain: Indolence can also mean a relaxed, slow state, especially on a hot day. «Summer indolence filled the village: the dogs slept in the shade and nobody hurried.» Nobody hurried and the dogs slept, so indolence here is not about being lazy in a bad way. It is a calm, sleepy feeling.
explain: Try the swap check. Put «laziness» in place of indolence: «Summer laziness filled the village» still makes sense, so you have the right meaning. Remember that indolence is uncountable: you say «a life of indolence», not «an indolence».
fact: Indolence is a noun, and it is uncountable: say «a life of indolence», not «an indolence».
fact: «Indolence» means laziness, or not wanting to work or make an effort.
fact: «Indolence» can also mean a relaxed, slow state, as on a hot day: «summer indolence».
fact: «Indolence» is a formal word, found in stories and old books; in everyday talk say «laziness».
fact: Phrases to know: «a life of indolence», «sheer indolence», «summer indolence».
exercise: «He spent the whole summer in indolence, reading under a tree.» What does indolence mean here? | Hard work and great energy | Anger at the weather | *Laziness, not wanting to make an effort | Loud singing
exercise: Which everyday word is closest in meaning to indolence? | Kindness | Speed | Curiosity | *Laziness
exercise: «Summer indolence filled the village: the dogs slept in the shade and nobody hurried.» What does summer indolence suggest? | A busy market day | *A slow, relaxed, lazy feeling on a hot day | A storm coming | A race in the park
test: «The prince's indolence meant the castle roof was never mended.» What does indolence mean? | *Laziness and not making an effort | Sudden fear | Spare time for games | Fast and careful work
test: Which everyday word would you use in speech instead of indolence? | Hunger | *Laziness | Energy | Kindness
exam: «Sheer indolence kept the old man in his chair all day, though the fire had gone out.» What does indolence mean here? | Great energy | Quiet thought | Hunger | *Laziness, not wanting to make an effort
